      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* Water molecules have a \"bent\" shape. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. \n* This polarity is what makes water the **\"universal solvent\"**—it can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid, which is crucial for chemical and biological processes.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nUnder normal conditions on Earth (room temperature and standard pressure), water is a liquid. However, it is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n* **Liquid:** Water as we normally drink it, flowing in rivers, rain, and oceans.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water (ice) is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, or steam from boiling water.\n\nWater also has a very high **heat capacity**, meaning it can absorb and release large amounts of heat. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable in living organisms.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* It is constantly moving through the **water cycle** (hydrological cycle): evaporating from the surface, condensing into clouds, falling back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flowing back into the oceans.\n* It shapes the Earth's landscape through erosion, carving canyons, shaping coastlines, and wearing down mountains over millions of years.\n\n### 4. The"
